From: Project Arctic
Subject: kernelbase: process snapshots capture threads and handles, and can be walked.

PssCaptureSnapshot, PssQuerySnapshot and PssFreeSnapshot were stubs that
failed, and the walk (PssWalkSnapshot, PssWalkMarker*) was not exported at
all: Logitech G HUB's agent imports PssWalkSnapshot, PssWalkMarkerCreate
and PssWalkMarkerFree, and did not start (c0000135), so its installer
waited for it for ever. A snapshot now holds the process's information
(basic, times, memory counters, image name), its threads (from the
system's process list) and its handles (from the system's handle list,
with their type, basic counts and name when asked for), kept in the
calling process until it is freed; a walk marker goes through threads or
handles as Windows' does. The address space is not cloned: its walks end
at once (ERROR_NO_MORE_ITEMS).
